{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-341255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is the value of $f(-3)?$\n\n$f(-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}+2(-x)+3 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}-2(-3)+3=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aad8f09049d423367798d634c28a5568a9b65da839c2ae5f564abbf9c448150b"} {"question" "q-341255" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341255" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aad8f09049d423367798d634c28a5568a9b65da839c2ae5f564abbf9c448150b"} {"question" "q-341243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jak