e{cos}⁡(nx)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341267" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)=x+4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=x+4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((\\pi +4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)-(0+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx))+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aad8f09049d423367798d634c28a5568a9b65da839c2ae5f564abbf9c448150b"} {"question" "q-341266" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/state.json" "file_sha256" "a8ef3971ca071ae0b4af2a88138fe25a2bbd413fee03d78a5f880160968cf41e"} {"question" "q-341266" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}"] ["math" "\\frac{1-(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}"] ["math" "\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}"]